description: >
  Ship the brainBackends registry — a single-table dispatcher that maps MCP
  entry shapes to backend drivers, exposing resolveBackend and getDriverById
  over one matcher table with an obsidian matcher and instruction template.
intent: >
  Ship the brainBackends registry — a single-table dispatcher mapping wired
  MCP entry shapes to backend drivers, exposing `resolveBackend(entry)` and
  `getDriverById(id)` over one matcher table and shipping today's lone
  obsidian matcher with its entry-point reach prose.
---

## Project Structure

```
src/service/brainBackends/
├── .aide           ← [existing] intent spec for this orchestrator
├── plan.aide       ← [this file] implementation plan
├── index.ts        ← [new] orchestrator + matcher table + obsidian matcher/template
│                     exports:
│                       default function resolveBackend(entry: McpServerEntry): BackendDriver | null
│                       function getDriverById(id: string): BackendDriver | null
│                       type re-exports of BackendDriver, McpServerEntry from @/types
└── index.test.ts   ← [new] co-located tests covering both accessors,
                      the obsidian command/args predicate, and the rendered
                      prose contract
```

This module ships exactly two `.ts` files. The single-helper exception in
the progressive-disclosure note applies here: today's surface is one
matcher predicate plus one prose template — small enough that a helper
subfolder would add ceremony without payoff. The strategist's forcing
function is "factor a backend into a sibling subfolder when its matcher
or template grows past ~50 lines" (per the spec's body); today the
obsidian matcher and template together fit comfortably under that
threshold, so they live inline. When a second backend lands, the
implementor following that plan re-evaluates the layout against the
same threshold.

No `.aide` next to helpers — there are no helpers to spec; the
orchestrator's `.aide` is the single source of truth for this module's
intent.

## Prerequisites

The shared types `BackendDriver` and `McpServerEntry` must already exist in
`src/types/index.ts` before this plan runs. Their definition is the
responsibility of the consumer's plan (`src/tools/brain/plan.aide`,
step 1) — that plan adds them as a single edit to the central types
module. This plan does NOT redefine those types; it imports them from
`@/types/index.js` and re-exports them through this module's
`index.ts`.

The expected shapes (per the consumer's plan and this module's spec):

- `McpServerEntry` is `{ command: string; args: string[] }` — the shape
  found under `mcpServers[<key>]` in `.mcp.json`.
- `BackendDriver` is
  `{ id: string; renderInstructions: (state: { vaultPath: string }) => string }`.

Step 1 of this plan opens with a sanity check that those types are
present in `@/types/index.js`. If absent, the implementor must escalate
back to the architect rather than redefining the types here — defining
shared types inside a service module would violate the "types
centralized in `src/types/`" convention and fork the contract.

## Plan

### 1. Sanity-check the shared types in `@/types/index.js`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] 1. Open `src/types/index.ts` and confirm both `BackendDriver` and
      `McpServerEntry` are exported with the shapes above (per the
      consumer's plan step 1). If either is missing or shaped
      differently, STOP and escalate back to the architect — do NOT
      redefine the types inside `src/service/brainBackends/`. Centralized
      types are a shared boundary the registry consumes, not declares.

### 2. Implement the obsidian matcher predicate, instruction template, and matcher table inside `index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] 2a. Create `src/service/brainBackends/index.ts`. Imports:
        - `type { BackendDriver, McpServerEntry } from "@/types/index.js"`.
        Re-export both types from this module so consumers can write
        `import type { BackendDriver } from "@/service/brainBackends/index.js"`
        without reaching back into `@/types`. Re-exports use the
        `export type { … }` form (preserves the type-only surface, no
        runtime emission).
- [x] 2b. At module scope, declare a pure synchronous predicate
      `function matchesObsidian(entry: McpServerEntry): boolean`. The
      predicate inspects the entry's `command` and `args` shape against
      the launcher signature `provisionBrain` writes today
      (`src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts` — `obsidianMcpEntry`).
      Both platform shapes must match:
        - **POSIX shape:** `command === "npx"`,
          `args.length >= 2`, and `args[0] === "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"`.
        - **Windows shape:** `command === "cmd"`,
          `args.length >= 4`, `args[0] === "/c"`, `args[1] === "npx"`,
          and `args[2] === "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"`.
      Use a strict (exact-string) match on the package name —
      `"@bitbonsai/mcpvault"` only, no `includes`/`startsWith` heuristic.
      The strategist's load-bearing rule (spec body, "Matchers
      pattern-match on the entry's command/args shape, not on the MCP
      key") makes the package the unambiguous identity signal; loose
      matching would dilute that. Defensive shape checks for
      `Array.isArray(entry.args)`, `typeof entry.command === "string"`,
      and the position-by-position string comparisons stay inside this
      predicate so neither caller has to revalidate the shape. The
      predicate has no I/O, no async, no side effects — per the
      strategist's "predicates are pure synchronous functions" rule.
      JSDoc names which platform shape each branch covers and references
      `provisionBrain.obsidianMcpEntry` as the upstream that produces
      these shapes.
- [x] 2c. At module scope, declare a `const OBSIDIAN_INSTRUCTION_TEMPLATE`
      function — `(state: { vaultPath: string }) => string`. The function
      returns a multi-sentence prose block that:
        1. Names the MCP read tool by its server-namespaced identifier
           (`mcp__brain__read_note`) — never `mcp__obsidian__*`. Per the
           handoff invariant, every brain-class entry is keyed `brain`
           upstream so agents only ever see `mcp__brain__*` tool names.
        2. Names the seeded entry-point file path:
           `<vaultPath>/CLAUDE.md` (the file `provisionBrain` writes via
           `VAULT_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE`).
        3. Says in one sentence what the file contains (navigation
           rules) **without enumerating any of its content** — no
           folder structure, no wikilink protocol, no
           where-to-find-things table, no decision protocol, no write
           rules. Those are the seeded `CLAUDE.md`'s job to own.
        4. Closes with a "the brain takes over from here and this
           server has no further role in your navigation" handoff
           sentence — verbatim spirit of the spec's "Good examples"
           Obsidian prose.
      The string must always be non-empty (single-pass concatenation;
      no conditional branches that could yield an empty result). The
      `vaultPath` is interpolated as-is — no normalization, no
      trailing-slash trimming. JSDoc names this as "the obsidian
      driver's `renderInstructions` function" and points to the spec's
      undesired-outcome line that forbids enumerating folder structure
      or write rules.
- [x] 2d. At module scope, declare a `const OBSIDIAN_DRIVER: BackendDriver`
      assembled from `id: "obsidian"` and
      `renderInstructions: OBSIDIAN_INSTRUCTION_TEMPLATE`. This is the
      single source of the driver — both accessors return the **same
      object reference** for the obsidian backend, never a fresh
      object per call. Reference identity is the structural guarantee
      the strategist's "two views over a single matcher table" rule
      pays off: a future test like
      `resolveBackend(obsidianEntry) === getDriverById("obsidian")`
      collapses accessor-drift into a single referential equality
      check.
- [x] 2e. At module scope, declare the matcher table:
      `const MATCHERS: ReadonlyArray<{ matcher: (entry: McpServerEntry) => boolean; driver: BackendDriver }>`.
      Initial contents: a single entry
      `{ matcher: matchesObsidian, driver: OBSIDIAN_DRIVER }`. Use
      `as const` on the array literal so TypeScript pins the shape.
      JSDoc explains: the array is the single source both accessors
      walk; adding a backend = appending one entry; order matters only
      if two predicates could ever fire on the same entry shape, which
      today they cannot (one entry total). When a second backend
      lands, the implementor following that plan revisits this rule.

### 3. Implement `resolveBackend(entry)` as the default export

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`

- [x] 3a. Below the matcher table in the same `index.ts`, declare
      `export default function resolveBackend(entry: McpServerEntry): BackendDriver | null`.
      The orchestrator walks `MATCHERS` and returns the first entry
      whose `matcher(entry)` returns `true`, returning that entry's
      `driver`. If no matcher fires, return `null`. Use early returns
      per the conventions' "Early Returns Over Nesting" rule — a
      `for…of` loop over `MATCHERS` with an inline `if (matcher(entry)) return driver;`
      and a final `return null` is the canonical shape.
- [x] 3b. Multi-line JSDoc on the default export naming the spec's
      load-bearing contract: returns the resolved driver for any
      command/args-shape this registry recognizes; returns `null` for
      every unrecognized wiring (no throw, no default-backend
      fallback, no key-based dispatch). Cite the spec's
      `outcomes.undesired` line forbidding throws — the contract is
      structured (driver-or-null), not exception-based.

### 4. Implement `getDriverById(id)` as a named export reading the same matcher table

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [x] 4a. Below `resolveBackend`, declare
      `export function getDriverById(id: string): BackendDriver | null`.
      The function walks `MATCHERS` and returns the first entry whose
      `driver.id === id`, returning that `driver`. If no entry
      matches, return `null`. **Walk the same `MATCHERS` array the
      resolver walks** — never declare a parallel `Map<string,
      BackendDriver>` or a separate registration list. This is the
      structural guarantee for the strategist's "every driver
      returned by the resolver is retrievable by id" rule: by reading
      from the same data, the two accessors cannot drift. JSDoc
      explains the symmetry and cites the spec's `outcomes.undesired`
      line forbidding accessor inconsistency.
- [x] 4b. Verify by reading both function bodies that
      `OBSIDIAN_DRIVER` is the only path either accessor returns for
      the obsidian id — same object reference, not a clone or rebuild.
      The strategist's "single matcher table = no drift" rule rests on
      this; an inline `// reference identity matters here` comment on
      the matcher-table declaration earns its keep when a future
      maintainer reaches for a `Map.get` shortcut.

