## Decisions

**Matcher table shape: inline `const` array of
`{ matcher, driver }` records, walked linearly.** Rejected: a
function-style registry (`registerBackend(driver, matcher)` called
from a side-effect-laden bootstrap) or a lazy/async loader. Why the
const array: today there is one matcher; lazy loading is ceremony
without a payoff and would force `resolveBackend` to be `async`
(violating the strategist's "predicates are pure synchronous
functions" rule and the wider "registry runs entirely on the data
the detector already has in hand" point). The const array is
inspectable from a debugger, immutable in `as const` form, and
trivially extensible — appending one record adds a backend. When the
roster grows past five-or-so backends or a backend's matcher needs
to load configuration, revisit; today the data structure is a list,
so the code is a list.

**Obsidian instruction template lives inline in `index.ts` as a
module-scoped function constant, not in a separate `obsidianTemplate.ts`
helper.** Rejected: a sibling subfolder
`src/service/brainBackends/obsidian/{ matcher.ts, template.ts }`. Why
inline: today's prose is a single multi-sentence string and the matcher
is a single shape check — together comfortably under the strategist's
~50-line factor-out threshold (spec body, "matchers and instruction
prose are co-located inside this module … factor [a] backend into a
sibling subfolder when [its] matcher predicate or prose template grows
past roughly fifty lines"). Splitting today would create a folder per
backend with two single-export files — exactly the empty-shell layout
the strategist's "factor by per-backend complexity, not by type of
content" rule rules out. When obsidian's matcher or template grows
past the threshold (or a second backend lands and the inline shape
starts crowding `index.ts`), the implementor following that plan
factors `obsidian/` out as a sibling subfolder containing its
matcher and template; the matcher table in the orchestrator stays
the same.

**Module file layout: lean `index.ts` only — no helper subfolder.**
Rejected: an `obsidian/` helper subfolder under
`src/service/brainBackends/` containing `obsidian/index.ts` plus
`obsidian/index.test.ts`. Why the lean shape: with one backend, the
helper subfolder would contain a single helper that the orchestrator
exports, which is the single-helper exception the
progressive-disclosure note explicitly calls out (helper goes inline
above the default export, not in a subfolder). The `index.ts` here
maintains the orchestrator-on-bottom rule (matcher predicate and
template above the default export, accessors below or at the bottom)
so the file reads top-to-bottom in dependency order without forcing
the reader to chase a folder for a one-record table. Re-evaluate
the layout when a second backend lands.

**`getDriverById(id)` walks the same `MATCHERS` array the resolver
walks — not a parallel `Map<string, BackendDriver>`.** Rejected:
build a `Map` at module load time keyed by `driver.id` for O(1)
lookups. Why the linear walk: structural drift prevention is the
load-bearing reason for the dual-accessor surface (spec
`outcomes.desired`, "every driver returned by `resolveBackend` is
retrievable by its id"). Two data structures cannot stay in lockstep
without test scaffolding that proves they do — and the strategist's
spec body explicitly calls this out: "the accessors must derive from
a single matcher table so they cannot disagree." A linear walk over
a single-digit-length array is O(N) but with N never approaching a
size where the cost matters — backends are added by humans editing a
matcher table, not at runtime. The reference-equality test in step
7b makes the symmetry guarantee load-bearing; a parallel `Map`
would either pass that test by coincidence (Map values point at the
same `OBSIDIAN_DRIVER` object) or fail it loudly (Map values are
clones), and neither outcome justifies the second data structure.

**Obsidian matcher's command/args predicate: strict exact-string
match on `"@bitbonsai/mcpvault"` at a fixed positional index per
platform shape.** Rejected: a permissive
`args.some(a => a.includes("mcpvault"))` heuristic, or a regex-based
match. Why strict: the spec body's load-bearing decision is
"identity comes from what the entry actually launches" and the
strategist names the package as the unambiguous identity signal.
Loose matching dilutes that — it would match a hypothetical fork
`@some-org/mcpvault-clone` that does not implement the same MCP
surface, silently rendering obsidian-shaped prose for a non-obsidian
backend. The platform-shape branch (POSIX `npx`-prefixed,
Windows `cmd /c npx`-prefixed) mirrors `obsidianMcpEntry` in
`provisionBrain/index.ts` exactly; both shapes are pinned in the
matcher predicate and tested in step 5c so a `provisionBrain` change
would surface as a registry test failure (and vice-versa). When a
real fork or alternative obsidian launcher exists, it gets its own
matcher entry and its own driver — never a loose match on the
existing one.

**`BackendDriver` and `McpServerEntry` ownership stays with
`src/types/index.ts` — re-exported from this module's `index.ts`,
not redefined here.** Rejected: define the types inside
`src/service/brainBackends/index.ts` and have `src/types/index.ts`
re-export from this module. Why central-types ownership: the spec's
desired outcome explicitly pins this — "the `BackendDriver` and
`McpServerEntry` types are defined here and re-exported via
`src/types/index.ts`." The wording in the spec body reads "defined
here" with `here` referring to the central project boundary
(`src/types/index.ts`), not the registry module — because every
shared definition the project carries lives in `src/types/`. The
reference list at the bottom of the spec confirms this:
"`BackendDriver` and `McpServerEntry` type shapes the registry's
**central-types declaration** carries." The consumer's plan
(`src/tools/brain/plan.aide`, step 1) is the canonical home for the
type-add edit; this plan's prerequisites declare that edit must
land first and step 1 of this plan sanity-checks for it. The
registry re-exports for consumer ergonomics — a downstream module
that imports the matcher should not have to reach back into
`@/types` for the types the matcher consumes.

**No `.aide` spec next to helpers.** No helpers exist as their own
folders today, so this is moot in shape — but worth noting for the
future maintainer who factors out `obsidian/` once the threshold is
crossed: the `.aide` rule is "next to orchestrators only." The
factored helper folder gets no spec; the orchestrator's `.aide` (this
file's sibling) remains the single source of truth for the module.

**Tests use the public surface, not the internal `OBSIDIAN_DRIVER`
constant.** Rejected: import `OBSIDIAN_DRIVER` directly from
`index.ts` (which would require exporting it) and assert against it.
Why the indirection: tests should pin the contract a downstream
consumer sees, not the implementation detail. `resolveBackend(entry).renderInstructions`
is the surface the brain tool calls; the tests exercise that exact
path. The reference-equality test in step 7b is the one place
internal-shape leaks into the tests — and that leak is intentional,
because the structural-drift guarantee is the contract.

## Follow-ups

None block this plan. The downstream consumer
(`src/tools/brain/plan.aide`) and the lifted detector
(`src/service/buildBrainState/plan.aide`) execute after this plan
ships — both are declared in the consumer plan's `## Follow-ups`
already.

When a second backend (Notion, GitHub Wiki, hosted PKB) is added in
a future spec round, the implementor revisits two structural choices
in light of the new content: (1) whether the obsidian matcher and
template still fit inline under the ~50-line threshold, factoring
out into `obsidian/` if not; (2) whether the matcher table's linear
walk still suits the new roster size — almost certainly yes for a
five-or-fewer-backend world, but the decision is worth re-examining
when the table grows. Both are roster-shaped revisions; neither
changes the contract this module exposes today.
